---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import StatTile from "../../components/StatTile.astro";
import Disclosure from "../../components/Disclosure.astro";
---

<Base
  title="The business that kept running after I left · Sahil Gaji"
  description="A food-and-beverage startup, co-founded alongside a full-time job. Honest account, not a success story."
>
  <Header current="work" />
  <main>
    <p class="eyebrow">Club House · Jul–Dec 2020</p>
    <h1>The business that kept running after I left.</h1>

    <div class="metrics" data-reveal>
      <StatTile value="6 months" label="part-time, alongside a full-time job" />
      <StatTile value="~ 40%" label="increase in online orders" definition="Following targeted social media campaigns and customer engagement work." />
      <StatTile value="₹600,000" label="funding secured" definition="From negotiated vendor and investor partnerships." />
      <StatTile value="~ 20%" label="increase in business valuation" />
    </div>

    <section class="prose">
      <p>
        A small food-and-beverage startup, co-founded with two friends
        alongside a full-time job. Breakfast and all-day snacks, out of
        Pune. Nothing glamorous — the goal was to keep the books straight,
        keep inventory from walking out the door, and keep customers coming
        back, on evenings and weekends around a day job.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built Excel VBA tools for invoicing, ingredient and stock
        tracking, and financial forecasting; ran the social media campaigns
        that grew online orders by roughly 40%; and negotiated the vendor
        and investor relationships that brought in ₹600,000 in funding.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="inversion" data-reveal>
      The business kept running after I left. Which either means the systems
      held up, or that I wasn't as essential as I liked to think while I was
      there. Probably both — and I'd rather build for the first outcome
      every time.
    </p>

    <h2>What actually happened</h2>
    <section class="prose">
      <p>
        After six months, ownership transitioned to my two co-founders. That
        wasn't the plan failing — a full-time job and a part-time startup
        were never going to both get the time they needed indefinitely, and
        we'd talked about that trade-off from the start. What mattered was
        whether the business could survive the handover: whether invoicing,
        stock tracking, and vendor relationships were documented and
        repeatable, or whether they only worked because I was the one doing
        them.
      </p>
      <p>
        They were repeatable enough. The business carried on without me.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Disclosure label="What I'd have built first, knowing this">
      <p>
        We built the boring tools — the invoicing and stock-tracking
        spreadsheets — reactively, once the manual version started breaking
        under its own weight. In hindsight, that's backwards: the boring
        infrastructure is exactly what should exist before a business needs
        it, not after. Marketing and growth are more fun to work on, so
        that's where effort drifted first.
      </p>
    </Disclosure>

    <h2>What I'd do differently</h2>
    <div class="retro" data-reveal>
      <p>
        If I could do it again, I would invest more time in documenting
        operational processes from the beginning instead of creating them
        reactively as the business grew. We solved problems as they
        appeared, but many of them could have been prevented with clearer
        systems earlier. That experience taught me that sustainable
        operations are built before they become necessary.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Verify this</h2>
    <ul class="verify">
      <li>Club House is listed on my CV, with the same dates and metrics.</li>
      <li>My former co-founders ran the business for longer than I did and are the better source for how it held up after the handover — happy to connect you if it's relevant to a conversation.</li>
    </ul>
  </main>
  <Footer lang="en" />
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-3);
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5.5vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-weight: var(--font-display-weight);
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-display-weight);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .metrics {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }
  .prose p {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .inversion {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.2vw, 2rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .retro {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-line);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .retro p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .verify {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .verify li {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
